{{smaller block|⟦200⟧When <math>\text{H}^\beta</math> was translated we have no definite means of determining. It was in all likelihood done subsequently to <math>\text{H}^\alpha</math>. The translator of <math>\text{H}^\beta</math> appears to have had the translation of <math>\text{H}^\alpha</math> before him, and to have followed it generally unless where there were manifest divergencies between <math>\text{H}^\alpha</math> and <math>\text{H}^\beta</math>.<sup>1</sup>}}

{{smaller block|⟦300⟧''Jewish Additions to the Text.''—(''a'') A large body of these additions can be classed under one head as written with a well-defined object and at a definite period. This period was about 70–40 {{sc|b.c.}}, and the object of the additions was the overthrow of the Maccabean high-priesthood, which in the 1st century {{sc|b.c.}} had become guilty of every lewdness. T. Lev. x., xiv.–xvi.; T. Jud. xvii. 2–xviii. 1 (?), xxi. 6–xxiii., xxiv. 4–6; T. Zeb. ix.; T. Dan. v. 6–7, vii. 3 (?); T. Naph. iv.; T. Gad. viii. 2; T. Ash. vii. 4–7. These additions are identical in object and closely related in character and diction with the Psalms of Solomon.}}

{{smaller block|⟦700⟧(''b'') Other additions are of various dates and cannot be more than mentioned here, ''i.e.'', T. Reub. ii. 3–iii. 2; T. Lev. xvii. 1–9; T. Zeb. vi. 4–6, vii.–viii. 3; T. Jos. x. 5–xviii.}}

{{smaller block|⟦700⟧''Christian Additions to the Text.''—These additions are to be found in most of the Testaments and were made at different periods. The existence of these Christian elements in the text misled nearly every scholar for the past four hundred years into believing that the book itself was a Christian apocryph. To Grabe, Schnapp and Conybeare belongs the credit of showing that the Christian elements were interpolations—to Conybeare especially of the three, since, whereas the two others showed the high probability of their contention on internal evidence, Conybeare proved by means of the Armenian Version that when it was made many of the interpolations had not yet found their way into the text. For a full treatment of these passages see R. H. Charles's ''Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs'' (1908), Introd. § 20.}}

{{smaller block|⟦1200⟧''Influence on the New Testament.''—We have already shown that St Paul twice quoted from the Greek text of the Testaments. These two passages in Rom. and 1 Thess. give but the very faintest idea of the degree of his indebtedness in thought and phraseology in several of his Epistles, especially that to the Romans. But of still greater interest are the passages in the Gospels which show the influence of the Testaments, and these belong mainly to the sayings and discourses of our Lord. We may mention two of the most notable of these. Thus Matt. xviii. 15, 35, which deal with the great question of forgiveness, are clearly dependent on our text.
{|
|Matt. xviii. 15. Ἐὰν δὲ ἁμαρτήσῃ ὁ ἀδελφός σου κατά σου, ὕπαγε ἔλεγξον αὐτὸν μεταξὺ σοῦ καὶ αὐτοῦ μόνου.||T. Gad. vi. 3. Ἐάν τις ἁμαρτήσει εἰς σέ εἰπὲ αὐτῷ ἐν εἰρήνῃ . . . καὶ ἐὰν . . . μετανοήσῃ ἄφες αὐτῷ.
|-
|35. Ἐὰν μὴ ἀφῆτε ἕκαστος τῷ ἀδελφῷ αὐτοῦ ἀπὸ τῶν καρδιῶν ὑμῶν.||vi. 6. Ἡσύχασον μὴ ἐλέγξῃς. . . .<br>v. 7. Ἄφες αὐτῷ ἀπὸ καρδίας.
